To implement a multiple-choice dialogue in a text-based Java game, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a class for the dialogue, which will contain the text and the choices. Here's a simple example:

```java
public class Dialogue {
    private String text;
    private String[] choices;
    private int correctChoice;

    public Dialogue(String text, String[] choices, int correctChoice) {
        this.text = text;
        this.choices = choices;
        this.correctChoice = correctChoice;
    }

    public String getText() {
        return text;
    }

    public String[] getChoices() {
        return choices;
    }

    public int getCorrectChoice() {
        return correctChoice;
    }
}
```

2. Create a method in your game class to display the dialogue and get the user's choice:

```java
public int showDialogue(Dialogue dialogue) {
    System.out.println(dialogue.getText());
    for (int i = 0; i < dialogue.getChoices().length; i++) {
        System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + dialogue.getChoices()[i]);
    }

    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    int choice = scanner.nextInt();
    return choice - 1; // Adjust for 0-based index
}
```

3. Use the `showDialogue` method in your game loop to present dialogues to the user and check their answers:

```java
Dialogue dialogue = new Dialogue("What is the capital of France?", new String[]{"Paris", "London", "Berlin", "Madrid"}, 0);
int userChoice = showDialogue(dialogue);

if (userChoice == dialogue.getCorrectChoice()) {
    System.out.println("Correct!");
} else {
    System.out.println("Incorrect. The correct answer is " + dialogue.getChoices()[dialogue.getCorrectChoice()]);
}
```

